In the misty glades of Stranglethorn Vale, a young Gnome warlock named Tallyn rode his pet succubus, Liora, through the fog-laced woods. Liora’s golden eyes sparkled as she glided over the mossy ground, her soft purr echoing through the trees. Tallyn had just completed a quest to retrieve a stolen moonpetal from a rogue troll, and Liora’s *Succubus Charm* had made the encounter effortless—she lured the troll with a whisper, then distracted him long enough for Tallyn to finish the job. The quest log flashed: “+100 XP, +10 reputation.” Tallyn grinned. “You’re the best pet for leveling,” he said. “No need for voidwalkers.”  

But as he turned to leave, a shadow passed through the trees. A voidwalker stepped forward, eyes glowing with ancient power. “You’ve never used *Voidwalk*,” it said. “You’re missing the real path.”  

Tallyn blinked. “I didn’t know I needed it.”  

The voidwalker smiled. “You do. But you don’t *see* it. Because of *Succubus Charm*—it makes you forget the void.”  

**Answer:** The succubus’s *Succubus Charm* makes players feel more powerful and confident, leading them to believe they don’t need voidwalkers. In reality, voidwalkers are more useful for travel, but the charm creates a false sense of self-sufficiency—making players *perceive* succubuses as sufficient, even when they’re not. Hence, most warlocks *think* they’re using succubuses, but the riddle’s hint reveals the illusion. [end of text]


